      "url": "https://www.last.fm/music/Porcupine+Tree/Signify",
      "wiki_content": "Signify is the fourth studio album by British progressive rock band Porcupine Tree, first released in September, 1996. It was the first album that frontman Steven Wilson recorded with a full group of musicians on board from the beginning. Previously he had been recording albums primarily as a one-man band with help gradually from other musicians. A number of the songs (albeit in demo form) were debuted live on the The Sky Moves Sideways tour before being recorded. The song \"Every Home is Wired\" has been covered by Polish electronic group Cabaret and included in their single Electric Chair Song. The full quote from the background words of \"Idiot Prayer\" can be heard on the song Trippin' by SRM. <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Porcupine+Tree/Signify\">Read more on Last.fm</a>. User-contributed text is available under the Creative Commons By-SA License; additional terms may apply.",
      "wiki_summary": "Signify is the fourth studio album by British progressive rock band Porcupine Tree, first released in September, 1996. It was the first album that frontman Steven Wilson recorded with a full group of musicians on board from the beginning. Previously he had been recording albums primarily as a one-man band with help gradually from other musicians. A number of the songs (albeit in demo form) were debuted live on the The Sky Moves Sideways tour before being recorded. The song \"Every Home is Wired\" has been covered by Polish electronic group Cabaret and included in their single Electric Chair Song. <a href=\"https://www.last.fm/music/Porcupine+Tree/Signify\">Read more on Last.fm</a>."
    },
    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Released in September 1996 on Delerium Records, Signify is the fourth studio album by Porcupine Tree and a key turning point in the band’s evolution. It was the first Porcupine Tree album made with the full group involved from the start, rather than functioning largely as a Steven Wilson solo project, and that shift gives the record a more collaborative and band-oriented feel.\n\nMusically, the album blends progressive rock with psychedelic, space rock, and krautrock influences, moving between atmospheric instrumentals and more structured songs. Its mix of hypnotic repetition, heavy textures, and melodic detail helped define the band’s late-1990s sound, and it is often regarded as one of Porcupine Tree’s most distinctive early releases.",
